Infrastructure as Code
Infrastructure as Code (IaC) is no longer unfamiliar to DevOps professionals and stands as a cornerstone of Platform Engineering. A successful IaC is to achieve Self-service and GitOps, where Self-service enables target users — those requesting specific resources — to create them independently, without requiring intervention from DevOps teams.

Lately, there are many people advocating IDPs and Crossplane. I took an interest and spend sometime to investigate if the Crossplane’s Juice, is worth the squeeze.

Before you proceed, note that this article reflects my personal perspective on IaC tools. If you’re a strong advocate of Crossplane, you may want to stop here, if you are easily offended

How each IaC tool works in general
Terraform
Install a cli from terraform of a certain version. Terraform OpenTofu
Configure your access to your cloud provider.
Create a *.tf to install writing your code. Which in general, usually comprises of the following
- Setup a backend to store your state file.
- Define your cloud provider
- Start writing resource blocks to create your resources
You then do each of the steps in sequence:
- terraform init
- terraform validate (optional - for syntax validation)
- terraform plan
-terraform apply
# Define the Terraform backend
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    key            = "state/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-lock-table" # Optional: For state locking
    encrypt        = true
  }
}

# Define the provider
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

# Create an S3 bucket
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "example" {
  bucket = "my-example-bucket"

  # Enable versioning
  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }

  # Enable server-side encryption
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm = "AES256"
      }
    }
  }

  # Define public access block
  bucket_policy = <<EOT
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "PublicReadGetObject",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-example-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {
        "Bool": {
          "aws:SecureTransport": "false"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
EOT
}

# Output the bucket name
output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.example.bucket
}
