Other Points
Reconciliation
Reconciliation in infrastructure as code (IaC) tools like Terraform, OpenTofu, Pulumi, and Crossplane involves aligning the actual state of resources with the desired state defined in code. However, if resources are created outside of IaC processes, they won’t be managed or reconciled by these tools.

Organizations with strong DevSecOps practices often enforce policies, such as Service Control Policies (SCPs), to prevent resource creation outside approved IaC pipelines. This approach requires a mature engineering culture and adherence of compliance to actual practices.

Reconciliation processes typically involve querying cloud provider APIs to compare and align resource states. To manage the frequency of these operations and avoid issues like API rate limiting, it’s essential to adjust reconciliation rates and polling intervals.

Recommendations:

1. Adjust Reconciliation Rate:

Use the --max-reconcile-rate flag to set a global maximum rate of reconciliations per second across all controllers. See here.
Set the --poll-interval flag to determine how often controllers check the state of managed resources.
2. Configure Controller Settings:

Implement a ControllerConfig resource to pass arguments like --poll=5m, adjusting the polling frequency to reduce API call rates. See here.
While these issues were identified several years ago, they remain relevant for newcomers to Crossplane.

Regarding Terraform, automating reconciliation can be achieved by integrating it with continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines that trigger like a cronjob. This setup ensures that only things needed and safe to be reconcile, will reconcile.

Operational Safety
The terraform plan command is a crucial feature in Terraform's workflow, allowing you to preview proposed changes before applying them. This step is essential for ensuring that modifications won't inadvertently destroy or alter critical resources, such as production databases.

In my experience, I had use a Terraform provider from one of our service provider which shows update and no recreate when running terraform plan, however it recreates upon terraform apply thus losing data/setup of the thing managed through Terraform. We had the plan logs within our Ci/Cd and ask provider to investigate the issue. It was the official terraform provider from out service provider that is an issue.

Now without this feature, I would lack confidence in making changes to critical production resources, such as databases. This command is essential for previewing proposed modifications, ensuring that unintended actions — like accidental deletions or misconfigurations — are identified before implementation.

Conclusion
Terraform and OpenTofu are highly relevant to me, but much of today’s Terraform code lacks good DevEx and extensibility while failing to embrace the KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid) principle.

AWS Networking is an example I created, leveraging open-source tools with the flexibility to replace components if needed. It emphasizes security, compliance, modular “Lego block” designs for building solutions (e.g., EC2, S3, RDS, SQS/SNS), and FinOps features like cost estimation and upfront budget planning. Git serves as the “source of truth,” anchoring IDP, source code, documentation, and linking to dashboards and metrics.

At this point, I don’t see the value in investing hours of engineering effort to deviate from the foundation of an existing comprehensive Git tool, such as GitLab or Azure DevOps.

The autopilot system, whether driven by AI or automation, must always have a fail-safe mechanism where humans act as the ultimate fallback and last line of defense. Personally, I wouldn’t feel comfortable flying in an airplane without a human pilot — or with a human pilot who lacks basic controls like a yoke.

Here’s a real-world example: My security team once applied SCPs that inadvertently rendered our IaC runners unable to execute requests. Despite attempts to restore functionality, the solution ultimately required recreating an API key for the runners. During the outage, however, we were able to fall back on manual IaC operations, which our tooling had made straightforward and efficient.

Unless a quick and robust disaster recovery (DR) plan is in place, it’s hard to imagine having an effective interim Plan B to keep operations running smoothly for Crossplane.
